      "problem": "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14247879/assets/e4a6bad4e26a2f078930ca95d56999b315cfe55328367bbb1508333891a10618.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$",
